it's not really about what happens at  the end of the race it's really having  the courage to show up and to start and  to look at something that seems really  impossible but to break it down and not  only do it but also enjoy it and find a  new part of what I'm capable of  I worked at a hedge fund on Wall Street  for five years and I realized at one  point that I really needed an outlet I  felt like I was in a job where you know  my passion really started in the morning  when I was out riding my bike or running  or talking to people about it later in  the day for me my definition of balance  is that there really isn't balance  because I like to be 100 percent driving  toward what I find most fulfilling and  where I feel like I can make a  difference  I am the women's product manager at  specialized so my mission is to bring  more women into the sport of cycling at  the core I'm responsible for really  managing our women's product line all  designed to fit women and give women the  most enjoyable experience possible out  cycling when I meet women who are  curious about cycling one of my biggest  tasks is just to really spark that  curiosity and to bring them into a sport  that has really defined my life in so  many ways and is a passion that I live  every single day I was the youngest  woman to run a marathon and all seven  continents so that included 26.2 miles  across the Gold Coast in Australia  Florida and North America Brazil and  South America Dublin in Europe Mount  Kilimanjaro in Africa the Beijing  Marathon in Asia and then of course the  clinical experience which was the  Antarctica marathon way down at the  bottom with earth ultimately I think  when I finally got the record it had  been a quest for over two years and I  was so excited about it and so  passionate about it and I remember  getting the certificate and kind of  being like oh that's nice and what I was  really excited about were the people  that I met along the way  sort of memories I had of these places  and it just really opened up my  worldview in a way where when I got to  the end of that road the record was a  nice piece of paper but the process was  really real out of the learning happened  I thought that was one definition of  challenge  and the ironman opened up a whole new  world and Ironman is a journey that  takes you from a lake to a bike to a run  and every high and low in between and  there is something in the suffering of  Ironman and the communal suffering that  really pushed me forward it's been a  goal that I trained for and it's also  been something that's defined my life  it's a way that I like to live it's more  than a race it's an experience and a  lifestyle for me I don't think I could  do it unless I really enjoyed the  process readings on atop the Blue Nile  Gordon one of the things that I asked  myself a lot is what's next I decided to  apply to Stanford as a way to transition  into a fitness focused career that was  based more around my core passions I had  four months before I had to be at school  and what better way to fill four months  than to ride a bicycle from Cairo to  Cape Town so I entered the longest  bicycle stage race in the world I left a  great job great apartment great life in  New York City got on a plane to Cairo  almost walked up and asked the pilot to  turn the plane around and I didn't want  to go but as the plane was descending  into Cairo I realized that I was  embarking on something really special  I took a different route out of the GSB  the many of my classmate my opportunity  at specialize was very unexpected I came  in to this wonderful place with all  these bikes and people who love to bike  and after five years in finance it's  been just a really exciting new  challenge to work on products and to  work on products that I am personally a  very passionate about was great for us  as people that are passionate about  cycling right not that they like to bike  they love to bike and know how much of a  life changer it can be for people so  when we met Aaron that passion for  cycling but more importantly her drive  and determination in everything that she  had done in her life it was like well  she's fantastic  I don't think I could do my current job  without the two years I spent at the GSB  coming into an environment where  feedback is very direct it comes from  all over the world like the GSB  empowered me to prioritize my time to  listen to feedback and to really have  the ability to step into leadership  I think I've followed a very unusual  path I think it's just a part of my  philosophy which is always asking what's  next and always driving toward whatever  is around the next bend and not being  afraid to step into something very  uncomfortable for me it's the next  Ironman the next bike ride that's just  sort of moving forward constantly and in  every possible direction
 

